This round (2026-07-17, 7th pass): custom garment WORN on a realistic male/female
avatar, balloon→garment 3D reshape, and 2D↔3D↔AR size + placement parity — all
RESOLVED, verified headlessly (tsc, production build, ar-verify all cases incl. a
two-sided custom, inflate-verify, a direct 3D-mesh-width probe, worn-figure render
QA) and adversarially reviewed by a multi-agent pass (1 real bug found + fixed:
two-sided custom back decal was back-face-culled → invisible from behind).

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. Custom garment WORN by a male/female human model in AR             ✅ RESOLVED
   Was: the bare uploaded garment floated with no body; the gender selector was
   hidden for custom.
   Now: buildCustomAvatarFigure (arExport) drapes the customer's ACTUAL uploaded
   garment — the same photo+design composite 2D/3D use — onto the realistic avatar
   as a body-CONFORMING decal (buildConformedDecal raycasts a subdivided plane onto
   the torso, both curvatures), over the avatar's own garment recoloured to a
   neutral undershirt. Laid-flat SLEEVES are cropped (bodyCropFraction) so the
   avatar's own neutral sleeves show instead of flat flaps. Two-sided uploads show
   front AND back (the back conformed decal reverses winding + mirrors UVs so it
   faces, and reads correctly from, the rear — confirmed by a rear-view render).
   The gender selector is now shown for custom, sharing a persisted figureGender
   pref. Fallbacks: bare inflated shell → procedural mannequin. (A procedural-body
   worn figure was prototyped and abandoned — too crude next to the AI avatars.)

2. Custom garment BETTER 3D VOLUME (not a "balloon")                   ✅ RESOLVED
   silhouette.ts reshape: seamed cos() cross-section (front/back panels meet at a
   garment seam, not a sealed pillow), per-row MEDIAL depth (chest deep, sleeves/
   hem shallow), a flatter back, chest-biased vertical with tucked shoulders/hem,
   and a raised AO floor. inflate-verify curvature 0.544. The 3D editing preview
   shows this shell (design prominent); AR shows the same garment worn. Back-only
   uploads now face forward in the preview too.

3. 2D ↔ 3D ↔ AR math / proportions consistent                         ✅ RESOLVED
   Root cause of the size mismatch: the 3D catalog mesh scaled its WORN girth to
   the LAID-FLAT width (over-inflated ~20%), so a true-inch print read ~56% in 3D
   vs ~72% worn in AR. Fix: calibration.wornFactor (tee 0.80, hoodie 0.86) narrows
   girth X/Z ONLY — height preserved, so areaOffsetYIn / decalNudge / Floor /
   camera framing are untouched. Verified by a direct probe: tee 3D mesh → 17.20in.
   AR vertical: the print sat ~3in too high (garmentCenterFrac 0.72 crowded the
   collar) → GARMENT_CENTER_FRAC measured per (gender, garment); AR↔3D vertical now
   matches (parity vFrac spread 0.010). 2D stays the flat / DTG-platen reference by
   design; the print is drawn at its TRUE inch size in every view.

4. 3D shows the print CLEARLY + CORRECTLY                             ✅ RESOLVED
   AR decals now bake at 2048 (potCanvas was silently snapping 1400→1024 = soft
   walk-up AR); sleeve capped at 1024; 3D preview 2048. The catalog decal now
   multi-point surface-probes (centre + 4 mid-edges) and clamps its projector depth
   to ≤ 0.55·halfDepth, so tall prints no longer clip at the torso's vertical
   curvature or bleed the front print through to the back.

Known-inherent (not blockers): the procedural mannequin (fallback only, if the
avatar GLB can't load) is stylised; a custom upload with no clean cutout falls back
to the bare inflated shell (no body); very dark studio colours read near-black in
dim light. The parity-verify ratioW metric is noisy (sheen bloom over the black
readback bg) — trust its vertical vFrac and the direct mesh probe.
